--TEST--
A `|>` and the nesting it substitutes to answer the same over the four shapes a right side takes, and a chain reads left to right — `rule:expressions/pipeline-substitution`, ADR 0098's M1 verification line
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The *agreement* shape over the four right-side shapes
// `a_pipeline_produces_the_same_ast_as_the_nested_spelling` compares as trees:
// a call, an index, a member access and a parenthesized group. That test
// asserts the two spellings parse the same; this one asserts they *run* the
// same, which is the whole claim of `rule:expressions/pipeline-substitution` —
// and what is counted is how many of the four agreed, so a shape that grew its
// own answer fails here while still printing plausibly on its own line.
class Tag {
    public string $name;

    public function constructor(string $name) {
        $this->name = $name;
    }
}

string $padded = "  Padded  ";
array<string> $words = ["first", "second"];
Tag $tag = new Tag("tagged");
int $n = 21;

int $agreed = 0;

// A call — the shape the rule's own example writes.
if (($padded |> Core\Str::trim($_)) == Core\Str::trim($padded)) { $agreed = $agreed + 1; }
// An index.
if (($words |> $_[0]) == $words[0]) { $agreed = $agreed + 1; }
// A member access.
if (($tag |> $_->name) == $tag->name) { $agreed = $agreed + 1; }
// A parenthesized group — the right side is parsed at the postfix level, so
// anything that is not already a call or an access is written this way.
if (($n |> ($_ * 2)) == ($n * 2)) { $agreed = $agreed + 1; }

echo $agreed, " of 4 shapes agree\n";

// Left-associative, so a chain reads in the order it happens while the tree it
// becomes is the inside-out nesting.
echo "  hello  " |> Core\Str::trim($_) |> Core\Str::upper($_), "\n";
--EXPECT--
4 of 4 shapes agree
HELLO
